---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const title = 'FAQ | pregnancydiscriminationcalc.com';
const description = 'Frequently asked questions about pregnancy discrimination claims: PDA coverage, PWFA accommodations, EEOC filing, and damages.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>Frequently Asked Questions</h1>
  <h2>What does the Pregnancy Discrimination Act cover?</h2>
  <p>The PDA prohibits discrimination based on pregnancy, childbirth, or related medical conditions by employers with 15+ employees. It also requires employers to treat pregnancy-related conditions the same as other temporary disabilities — if an employer provides light duty for workers with lifting restrictions, it must provide the same for pregnant workers.</p>
  <h2>What is the Pregnant Workers Fairness Act?</h2>
  <p>The PWFA (effective June 2023) requires employers with 15+ employees to provide reasonable accommodations for known limitations related to pregnancy, childbirth, or related medical conditions, unless the accommodation causes undue hardship. This is modeled on the ADA and is broader than the PDA&rsquo;s equal treatment requirement.</p>
  <h2>Does the PDA protect nursing mothers?</h2>
  <p>The PUMP Act (2022) extended FLSA protections for nursing mothers: employers must provide reasonable break time and a private space (not a bathroom) for pumping for 2 years after the child&rsquo;s birth. Title VII may also protect against discrimination based on lactation in some courts.</p>
  <h2>What is the EEOC filing deadline?</h2>
  <p>180 days from the discriminatory act (300 days in states with a fair employment agency). After receiving a right-to-sue letter, you have 90 days to file in federal court. Missing the charge filing deadline bars your Title VII claim.</p>
  <h2>What if the employer says they fired me for performance?</h2>
  <p>Employers frequently assert performance as a pretext for pregnancy discrimination. Evidence of pretext: positive performance reviews before the pregnancy announcement, timing of termination relative to disclosure, replacement by a non-pregnant employee, different treatment of similarly situated non-pregnant employees. Pretext analysis is the core of most pregnancy discrimination cases.</p>
</BaseLayout>
